speechtext:
We cannot intervene, short of commiting an act of war against Nigeria and intervening in the affairs of that country. May [Mr. Stanfield.}
DEBATES	September 27, 1968
I repeat that 29 out of 33 members of the Organization for African Unity have refused to do so. These African states themselves have said they would not interfere in the affairs of Nigeria. It would be very wrong for Canada to interfere in the affairs of Nigeria and to send our own army and air force there, even under a humanitarian guise, to do something which would be a violation of that nation's frontiers. I hope that even if the opposition does not see that, the Canadian people will. If they want to be generous with their lives and their money, I wish to heck they would.

Resolved, that a sum not exceeding $3,776,197,303.75 being the aggregate of-
(a)	nine-twelfths of the total of all the items set forth in the revised main estimates, 1968-69, for the fiscal year ending the 31st of March, 1969, laid before the House of Commons at the present session of parliament, $4,852,052,274.75;
(b)	an additional two-twelfths of Energy, Mines and Resources item 60, Finance item 30, Legislation item 15, Public Works items 25 and 30, Secretary of State item 2, Transport items 15, 25 and 90, Treasury Board item 25, Loans, Investments and Advances items L35 and L125, (Schedule A) of the said revised main estimates, $35,666,416.67;



September 27, 1968
Interim Supply
(c)	an additional one-twelfth of Agriculture item 15, Energy, Mines and Resources item 75, Indian Affairs and Northern Development item 34, National Defence items 20 and 50, Privy Council item 15, Regional Development item 25, Secretary of State items 1 and 65, Transport item 5, (Schedule B) of the said revised main estimates, $6,681,409.50;
Less the amounts authorized by Appropriation Act No. 2, 1968, $1,118,202,797.17, be granted to Her Majesty on account of the fiscal year ending the 31st of March, 1969.
[DOT] (12:20 p.m.)
